neoliberalism is a very contested  concept and it's an odd concept because  it's the term neoliberalism is mostly  used by people who don't like it new  liberalism happened very suddenly it was  carrying a lot of new perspectives  unknown outcomes but everybody suspected  that it was going to be big there was an  important economic crisis in the 1970s  around the sometimes called the oil  crisis but it also led to high levels of  state deficit and debt and out of those  cried that crisis of the 1970s there  began to be a new set of political ideas  which said that the state is doing too  much it's spending too much the deficits  exist because we're spending too much  therefore we have to cut back the role  of the state neoliberalism represents a  break with previous ideas about the  relationship between markets and States  it's involves a considerable change  some people argue even revolutionary  change in the sorts of societies that  have developed in the West primarily  since the Second World War and so the  neoliberal reforms that started in the  in the late 70s and 80s  many people believe started to dismantle  some of the foundations of those  post-war successes at a political level  of course it reflects a set of policies  that are oriented towards deregulation  privatization making markets more  competitive at an economic level really  neoliberalism represents the opening of  markets the making of markets more  competitive many of the phenomena that  we associated with globalization  neoliberalism is important as a set of  ideas at the cultural level  it has brought to the fore new ways of  thinking about people thinking about the  worth of what they're doing and in many  ways it's changing what we could think  of as a very self concepts of ordinary  people individuals are expected to  become to be fully self-sufficient to  pull themselves by their britches and  are not supposed to count on the state  or anyone else to ensure that their  prosperity and it has tremendously  impacted in in different ways that many  societies in all continents we live in  societies that have much greater  inequality today than they did 10 or 20  years ago I think this is one of the  fundamental challenges we face if we  want to continue to be a successful  society we need we need to very directly  address these growing inequalities and  particularly their their social and  cultural consequences
 

